config UDC_DWC3
	bool "Synopsys USB device controller driver"
	default y
	select NOCACHE_MEMORY if ARCH_HAS_NOCACHE_MEMORY_SUPPORT
	select UDC_BUF_FORCE_NOCACHE
	select UDC_DRIVER_HAS_HIGH_SPEED_SUPPORT
	select UDC_DRIVER_HAS_SUPER_SPEED_SUPPORT
	depends on DT_HAS_SNPS_DWC3_ENABLED
	help
	  Synopsys DWC3 USB device controller driver.

if UDC_DWC3

config UDC_DWC3_EVENTS_NUM
	int "Number of events in the event ring buffer"
	default 16
	help
	  It is typically not needed to increase the number of events, but
	  if it were to happen due to some rare condition, increasing this
	  number could be useful.

config UDC_DWC3_TRB_NUM
	int "Number of buffers for each non-control endpoint"
	default 4
	range 3 32
	help
	  This is a general knob for the number of buffers present for each
	  endpoint. This does not affect control endpoints. The default is
	  enough for most conditions, but for performance and debug purposes,
	  increasing could reveal useful.

config UDC_DWC3_EP_STALL_LOG
	bool "Watchdog log for CPU IN endpoints that stop retiring buffers"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM
	help
	  Report the full ring fingerprint of any CPU-managed IN endpoint whose
	  tail buffer has been pending, unchanged, for longer than
	  UDC_DWC3_EP_STALL_LOG_MS.

	  The wedge and run-dry logs only fire on paths the driver recognises
	  as broken. An endpoint that simply stops making progress -- tail
	  buffer never retired, no completion event, no doorbell error -- is
	  otherwise silent. That is the "CDC ACM shell went quiet mid-output"
	  symptom, and it cannot be diagnosed with a shell command because the
	  shell is the casualty. This watchdog puts the evidence on the boot
	  console instead.

config UDC_DWC3_EP_STALL_LOG_MS
	int "Pending time before an endpoint is reported stalled (ms)"
	default 1500
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_STALL_LOG
	help
	  Must comfortably exceed the worst-case legitimate time a buffer can
	  sit armed. Only endpoints with a queued buffer are considered, and
	  OUT is excluded: an OUT endpoint armed with nothing to receive is
	  idle by design and indistinguishable from a wedged one.

config UDC_DWC3_IN_PARK_RECOVER
	bool "Recover an IN endpoint whose armed TRB is never fetched"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM && UDC_DWC3_IN_START_ENDXFER_ESCALATE
	help
	  Re-arm a CPU-managed IN endpoint whose tail TRB has stayed owned by
	  hardware, as the only owned TRB in the ring, for longer than
	  UDC_DWC3_IN_PARK_RECOVER_MS.

	  That state is what a dropped doorbell leaves behind, and no other
	  mechanism can see it: the completion poll only retires TRBs hardware
	  has released, no completion event is ever generated, and the run-dry
	  nudge is OUT-only. Without this the pipe stays dead permanently.

	  Recovery re-presents the same slot, which is safe on IN because the
	  transfer is device-paced and nothing has reached the wire yet.

config UDC_DWC3_IN_PARK_RECOVER_MS
	int "Time an IN TRB may stay unfetched before re-arming (ms)"
	default 250
	depends on UDC_DWC3_IN_PARK_RECOVER
	help
	  A healthy IN TRB is consumed within microseconds of the next host
	  token, so this only needs to be long enough to never fire on a
	  transfer that is merely waiting for the host to poll the endpoint.

config UDC_DWC3_EP0_RESCUE
	bool "Tick the EP0 recovery poll independently of event arrival"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_IN_COMPLETION_POLL
	help
	  Run the EP0 deferred-completion poll from the periodic worker as well
	  as at the end of each event batch.

	  EP0 defers a completion whose descriptor is still owned by hardware and
	  leaves the poll to retire it, but that poll only runs after an event
	  batch, so it cannot rescue anything unless another event arrives. When
	  the controller drops the follow-up completion and the bus is otherwise
	  quiet -- during UVC stream teardown and restart, for instance -- the
	  transfer stays stranded until the host times it out at five seconds,
	  which is the -110 seen on UVC probe control requests.

config UDC_DWC3_EP0_RESCUE_MS
	int "Interval for the independent EP0 rescue tick (ms)"
	default 10
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP0_RESCUE
	help
	  Keep this well below the host's control transfer timeout and well above
	  the bulk poll interval. Driving EP0 recovery at the bulk poll rate was
	  measured to starve the endpoint workers.

config UDC_DWC3_IN_INPROG_RETIRE
	bool "Retire CPU-managed IN transfers on XferInProgress"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM
	help
	  Treat XferInProgress as a completion for CPU-managed IN endpoints when
	  hardware has already released the tail descriptor.

	  This controller does not send XferComplete for those endpoints at all:
	  measured over a full streaming run, the ACM IN endpoint saw zero
	  XferComplete events and one XferInProgress per transfer. Discarding
	  XferInProgress therefore throws away the only completion signal there
	  is, and leaves every transfer to be recovered by the completion poll a
	  poll interval later -- which is what the poll, the endpoint state
	  machine and the park recovery all exist to paper over.

	  The ownership test means a burst within a still-owned descriptor is
	  ignored as before, so this does not reintroduce the premature retire
	  that made XferInProgress be ignored in the first place. Hardware-managed
	  endpoints are unaffected.

	  Turn off to restore the previous poll-dependent behaviour.

config UDC_DWC3_LOST_EVT_DIAG
	bool "Sample the event ring when the poll recovers a lost completion"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM && UDC_DWC3_IN_COMPLETION_POLL
	help
	  Read GEVNTCOUNT and the GEVNTSIZ interrupt mask each time the IN
	  completion poll retires a TRB that hardware released without posting an
	  event, and report the totals once a second.

	  This answers whether the missing events were never posted by the
	  controller or were posted and never collected by the CPU -- the former
	  is unfixable in the driver, the latter is a lost interrupt edge. Every
	  recovery path here was built without that distinction being known.

	  Diagnostic only; leave it off in production.

config UDC_DWC3_OUT_NOTREADY_RETAKE
	bool "Retake a bulk OUT transfer resource on XferNotReady"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_OUT_RUNDRY_ENDXFER_ESCALATE
	help
	  Enable the XferNotReady event on bulk OUT endpoints and, when one
	  arrives while the tail TRB is armed and hardware-owned, drop and retake
	  the transfer resource with EndXfer(ForceRM)+StartXfer.

	  XferNotReady means the host tried to send and HW had no transfer ready.
	  Seeing it against an armed TRB means HW is refusing data it has a
	  buffer for, which is a StartXfer doorbell that was accepted and then
	  dropped. Nothing else reports that condition: a wedged OUT endpoint and
	  an idle one are identical in the ring, so without this event the only
	  detector is a timer that cannot tell them apart and therefore has to
	  churn transfer resources on healthy endpoints to catch the sick ones.

config UDC_DWC3_OUT_STALL_REFRESH
	bool "Re-ring the doorbell on an OUT endpoint that stops progressing"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM && UDC_DWC3_OUT_RUNDRY_ENDXFER_ESCALATE
	help
	  Periodically reissue UpdateXfer on a CPU-managed OUT endpoint whose
	  tail slot has not moved for UDC_DWC3_OUT_STALL_REFRESH_MS, escalating
	  to EndXfer(ForceRM)+StartXfer once a few refreshes have changed nothing.

	  A class keeps a fixed number of OUT transfers armed and only arms a
	  replacement when one completes, so a single dropped completion caps it
	  permanently and host writes NAK forever. The run-dry path cannot catch
	  that (it needs a re-arm into a drained ring, which can no longer
	  happen) and neither can the completion poll (it needs HWO cleared).

	  UpdateXfer is a no-op on a healthy endpoint, so refreshing an idle
	  pipe is harmless; that matters because an armed OUT endpoint with no
	  host data is idle by design and cannot be told apart from a wedged one.

config UDC_DWC3_OUT_STALL_REFRESH_MS
	int "Time without OUT progress before re-ringing the doorbell (ms)"
	default 1000
	depends on UDC_DWC3_OUT_STALL_REFRESH
	help
	  Also the interval between refreshes, so the escalation to EndXfer lands
	  at a few times this value. Costs one endpoint command per interval per
	  idle OUT endpoint. Lowering it shortens a wedge but makes the escalation
	  race live host writes more often; raising it does the reverse.

config UDC_DWC3_HEALTH_LOG
	bool "Periodic USB health / buffer-leak logging"
	select NET_BUF_POOL_USAGE
	help
	  Periodically log DWC3 driver health: IRQ/event counters, control
	  endpoint busy state, and the usage of every net_buf pool (how many
	  buffers are currently claimed vs the maximum ever claimed).

	  This is a debugging aid to track down net_buf leaks that slowly
	  exhaust a pool and cause the host to re-enumerate the device after
	  it has been idle, without any processor crash. Watch the per-pool
	  "used" count: a pool whose "used" only ever grows (and never returns
	  toward 0 when the bus is idle) is the leaking pool.

	  The same data can be dumped on demand with `tvai_dwc3 mem <device>`.

config UDC_DWC3_HEALTH_LOG_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Interval between periodic USB health logs (ms)"
	default 5000
	depends on UDC_DWC3_HEALTH_LOG
	help
	  How often the periodic health snapshot is emitted, in milliseconds.

config UDC_DWC3_HEALTH_LOG_WARN
	bool "Emit the periodic health line at warning level"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_HEALTH_LOG
	help
	  Raise the periodic health line to warning level so it survives a
	  build whose driver log level is set to warning.

	  Every other line this driver emits is produced by USB activity, so a
	  console that goes quiet cannot distinguish a dead link from a dead
	  processor.  This line comes from a timer instead, which makes that
	  distinction: if it keeps arriving, the processor is running and the
	  link state it reports is the state the link is stuck in.

config UDC_DWC3_LINK_ESCAPE
	bool "Break out of a stuck SuperSpeed link by reconnecting"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_HEALTH_LOG
	help
	  Recover from a SuperSpeed link that has stopped carrying traffic
	  without failing outright.

	  The link can settle into alternating U0 and Recovery many times a
	  second: it retrains, errors immediately, and retrains again, so no
	  transfer ever completes.  Because it never reaches SS.Inactive the
	  host is never told anything is wrong -- the device stays enumerated
	  and its endpoints stay present while moving no data, which is why
	  only a power cycle clears it.

	  When the periodic health check sees events arriving with no transfer
	  and no control progress behind them, drop the run/stop bit briefly.
	  The host sees a disconnect and re-enumerates, so the link retrains
	  from scratch instead of staying in limbo.  This is a last resort
	  against an unrecoverable state, not a substitute for a link that
	  stays up.

config UDC_DWC3_LINK_ESCAPE_MAX
	int "Reconnect attempts allowed"
	default 5
	depends on UDC_DWC3_LINK_ESCAPE
	help
	  Give up after this many attempts, so a link that cannot be rescued
	  is left alone rather than bounced forever.

config UDC_DWC3_LINK_ESCAPE_DOWN_MS
	int "How long to hold the disconnect (ms)"
	default 200
	depends on UDC_DWC3_LINK_ESCAPE
	help
	  The device has to be absent long enough for the host to register a
	  disconnect rather than a glitch.

config UDC_DWC3_LINK_ESCAPE_COOLDOWN
	int "Health intervals to ignore after reconnecting"
	default 5
	depends on UDC_DWC3_LINK_ESCAPE
	help
	  Reset and connect events arrive with no transfers yet behind them,
	  which looks exactly like the wedge being detected.  Ignore that many
	  intervals after a reconnect so enumeration is not mistaken for a
	  wedge and interrupted by another one.

config UDC_DWC3_DMA_SLOT_DIAG
	bool "DMA slot usage tracking and IN scrub-at-enqueue"
	help
	  Track which 1 KiB NOCACHE slots (sram1) each bulk enqueue/completion
	  uses, log DMA-SLOT lines, memset 0xA5 on IN enqueue (debug builds only),
	  and dump slot history plus scrub fingerprints in stall_snapshot().

config UDC_DWC3_XFER_TRACE
	bool "Anomaly WRN logs for xfer_active / ring-reset / link-TRB hypothesis"
	help
	  Emit XFER-TRACE WRN lines when ring_reset or DepStartXfer runs while the
	  link TRB is still HW-owned, when xfer_active clears with link_hwo set,
	  or on other ring/HW desync suspects.  Intended for ACM+UVC soak debug.

config UDC_DWC3_WEDGE_LOG
	bool "Compact WEDGE snapshots on ACM/UVC concurrent failure paths"
	help
	  On rare wedge paths (empty TRB xfer-done, OUT run-dry verify exhausted,
	  etc.) emit a short per-EP ring summary and full stall_snapshot().  Much
	  less chatty than CONFIG_UDC_DWC3_XFER_TRACE for short soak iterations.

config UDC_DWC3_IN_COMPLETION_POLL
	bool "Periodic SW-retire poll to recover dropped IN completions"
	help
	  On this SoC the bulk IN XferComplete event is not always delivered to
	  the CPU event ring under multi-stream load: an armed IN TRB is filled
	  and released by hardware (HWO cleared) but no DEPEVT is posted, so the
	  transfer never retires and the endpoint wedges even though the CPU is
	  healthy.  Enable a light periodic poll that reclaims any endpoint whose
	  tail TRB had HWO cleared without a matching event, breaking the wedge.

	  The poll runs on the system workqueue, serialised with the event and
	  endpoint workers, and is a cheap per-endpoint HWO test when events are
	  delivered normally.

config UDC_DWC3_IN_COMPLETION_POLL_INTERVAL_US
	int "Interval between IN completion recovery polls (us)"
	default 500
	depends on UDC_DWC3_IN_COMPLETION_POLL
	help
	  How often the dropped-IN-completion recovery poll runs, in
	  microseconds.  Lower values reduce worst-case IN latency on a dropped
	  event at the cost of more workqueue wakeups.

config UDC_DWC3_EP_SM
	bool "HWO-ground-truth state machine for CPU bulk endpoints"
	default y
	select UDC_DWC3_IN_COMPLETION_POLL
	help
	  Unified per-endpoint state machine for CPU-terminated bulk endpoints
	  (ACM IN/OUT, etc.).  HWO on the tail TRB is ground truth; DEPEVT and
	  periodic poll are hints.  RTL-offloaded UVC IN endpoints bypass this.
	  Implies IN_COMPLETION_POLL for the shared periodic HWO poll worker.

config UDC_DWC3_EP_SM_HW_IN_EP_MASK
	hex "IN endpoints offloaded to the RTL video path"
	default 0x18
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM
	help
	  Bitmask of IN endpoint numbers whose transfers the RTL drives, so the
	  state machine must leave them alone: bit 3 means endpoint 0x83, bit 4
	  means 0x84, and so on.  The default 0x18 covers 0x83 and 0x84, the
	  video streams on a two-camera TinyCLUNX.

	  This has to match the actual descriptor layout.  An endpoint listed
	  here that a class terminates in software is excluded from the retire
	  poll, the tail watchdog and the XferInProgress retire, which together
	  are the only things that complete a transfer on a controller that
	  never raises XferComplete: hardware clears HWO, nothing retires the
	  buffer, and the pipe dies silently after one transfer.  An endpoint
	  left out of the mask that the RTL does drive gets managed twice.

config UDC_DWC3_EP_SM_LOG_UNMATCHED
	bool "Log DEPEVT without matching CPU-EP SM expectation"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM
	default n

config UDC_DWC3_EP_SM_LOG_PHASE
	bool "Log EP-SM enumeration phase transitions and premature SM activity"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM
	default n
	help
	  Emit WRN/INF lines when bulk_eps_live changes (enable, reset, first
	  bulk ep enable, last bulk ep disable) and when doorbell/advance/depevt
	  would touch CPU bulk endpoints before configuration.  Any premature
	  line during SET_ADDRESS is a smoking gun for enumeration wedges.

config UDC_DWC3_IN_START_ENDXFER_ESCALATE
	bool "Escalate a stranded IN StartXfer to EndXfer(ForceRM)+StartXfer"
	help
	  When an IN DepStartXfer doorbell is accepted but the controller never
	  fetches the owned TRB (confirmed on-core under two RTL-driven UVC IN
	  streams: the tail TRB stays HWO=1 and re-issued DepUpdateXfer commands
	  complete with no CMDERR yet do not resume it), the inline verify's
	  UpdateXfer retry budget is exhausted with the pipe still wedged.

	  Enable this to escalate that stranded-start case to the only remaining
	  databook lever: EndTransfer(ForceRM) to drop the parked transfer
	  resource, then a fresh StartXfer re-arming the same still-owned TRB.
	  This is safe only for IN (device-paced); the OUT path deliberately does
	  not use EndXfer+StartXfer because the restart races host-driven data.

config UDC_DWC3_OUT_RUNDRY_ENDXFER_ESCALATE
	bool "Escalate a stranded OUT run-dry to EndXfer(ForceRM)+StartXfer"
	depends on UDC_DWC3_EP_SM
	help
	  When an OUT bulk endpoint runs dry and parks, this IP silently drops the
	  DepUpdateXfer used to resume it (see udc_dwc3_out_rundry_restart): the
	  command completes with no CMDERR yet HW never re-fetches the owned TRB,
	  so the tail TRB stays HWO=1 and the pipe wedges permanently once the
	  UpdateXfer verify+spin budget is exhausted.  This is the EP-SM OUT-RUNDRY
	  failure observed on the CDC ACM shell-input endpoint under concurrent
	  RTL-driven UVC streaming.

	  Enable this to escalate that already-wedged case to EndTransfer(ForceRM)
	  + a fresh StartXfer re-arming the same still-owned TRB.  EndXfer+StartXfer
	  was historically avoided on OUT because a mid-stream restart can race
	  host-driven incoming data; this escalation fires ONLY from a confirmed
	  parked state (ring fully drained, no in-flight packet) and ONLY after the
	  UpdateXfer budget is spent, i.e. when the pipe is already dead.  It is
	  therefore strictly no-worse: it either re-fetches the TRB (pipe recovered)
	  or leaves the same wedged state.

endif # UDC_DWC3
